# Install and Configure the Authenticator App Extension in Microsoft Edge

## Overview

This guide explains how to:

1. Install the **Authenticator App** extension from the Chrome Web Store.
2. Enable the extension in Microsoft Edge.
3. Pin the extension to the Edge toolbar.
4. Add a new account to the extension.

The extension is available from:

- [Authenticator App - Chrome Web Store](https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/authenticator-app/bbphmbmmpomfelajledgdkgclfekilei)

The extension generates Time-based One-Time Passwords (TOTP) and can scan QR codes directly from web pages. 【1-a5a3f1】

---

# Part 1: Install the Authenticator App Extension

## Step 1: Open Microsoft Edge

1. Launch **Microsoft Edge**.
2. Ensure you are signed in with the appropriate profile if required.

## Step 2: Open the Chrome Web Store Page

1. In Edge, browse to:
   - [Authenticator App Extension](https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/authenticator-app/bbphmbmmpomfelajledgdkgclfekilei)
2. Review the extension details and publisher information. The extension is listed as **Authenticator app**. 【1-a5a3f1】

## Step 3: Allow Chrome Extensions (If Prompted)

If this is your first Chrome Web Store extension:

1. Edge may display a banner stating that extensions from other stores are blocked.
2. Select **Allow extensions from other stores**.
3. Select **Allow** to confirm.

## Step 4: Install the Extension

1. Select **Add to Chrome** on the Chrome Web Store page.
2. When prompted by Edge, select **Add Extension**.
3. Wait for the installation to complete.

A confirmation message will appear when the extension has been successfully installed.

---

# Part 2: Enable the Extension in Edge

## Step 1: Open the Extensions Menu

1. Select the **Extensions** icon in the Edge toolbar.
   - It appears as a puzzle-piece icon near the address bar.

Alternatively:

1. Select the **Settings and More** menu (**...**).
2. Select **Extensions**.
3. Select **Manage Extensions**.

## Step 2: Verify the Extension is Enabled

1. Locate **Authenticator App** in the list of installed extensions.
2. Confirm the toggle is set to **On**.

If it is disabled:

1. Turn the toggle **On**.
2. Close the Extensions page.

---

# Part 3: Pin the Extension to the Edge Toolbar

## Step 1: Open the Extensions Menu

1. Select the **Extensions** icon on the toolbar.

## Step 2: Pin the Extension

1. Locate **Authenticator App**.
2. Select the **Pin** icon next to the extension.

## Result

The Authenticator App icon will now be permanently visible on the Edge toolbar, making it easy to access authentication codes.

---

# Part 4: Add a New Account to the Authenticator App

## Option 1: Add an Account Using a QR Code

This is the most common method.

### Step 1: Start the Account Registration Process

1. Navigate to the website or service that is enabling Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
2. Choose the option to use an **Authenticator App**.
3. Display the QR code provided by the service.

### Step 2: Open the Extension

1. Select the **Authenticator App** icon from the Edge toolbar.

### Step 3: Add a New Entry

1. Select **Add Account**, **Add**, or the **+** button (depending on the extension version).
2. Choose the option to scan or import a QR code.

### Step 4: Capture the QR Code

If the extension offers QR scanning:

1. Select the scan option.
2. Click the displayed QR code on the webpage.

The extension will import the account details and create a new TOTP entry.

### Step 5: Verify the Registration

1. A six-digit code should appear in the extension.
2. Enter the displayed code into the website's MFA registration page.
3. Select **Verify**, **Continue**, or **Finish**.

The account is now registered.

---

## Option 2: Add an Account Manually

Use this method when the service provides a setup key instead of a QR code.

### Step 1: Obtain the Secret Key

1. On the MFA setup page, select:
   - **Can't scan?**
   - **Manual setup**
   - **Enter key manually**
   
   (wording varies by service)

2. Copy the secret key provided.

### Step 2: Open the Extension

1. Select the **Authenticator App** icon.

